# Contributing to cheshire-drivers

Thanks for your interest in cheshire-drivers. Please read the first section
before opening a pull request, because this repository does not work the way
most do.

## This repository is a snapshot

Development happens in a private working repository. What you see here is a
published snapshot of it, pushed at each release. Nothing is merged here
directly, and a branch you push here will be overwritten by the next snapshot.

That does not mean contributions are unwelcome. It means the path is:

1. Open a pull request here against `main`.
2. A maintainer reviews it here, in the open.
3. If it is accepted, the maintainer applies the change in the working
   repository with your authorship preserved, and it reaches this repository in
   the next snapshot.
4. Your pull request is then closed with a pointer to the commit that carries
   it.

So your PR is read, discussed and credited. It is just not the commit that
lands. Expect that to take longer than a normal merge.

Issues and feature requests work normally. Open them here.

## Before you open a pull request

- Keep it small and focused. A large change is more likely to be rejected for
  reasons of scope than of quality, and porting it by hand is harder.
- Say what problem it solves and how you verified it.
- If it changes behaviour, include a test that fails before your change and
  passes after.
- Open an issue first for anything that changes a driver interface. Orca and
  orca-client both build on these interfaces, so an interface change lands in
  all three at once.

## Contributor licence agreement

Contributing requires signing the
[Cheshire Labs Contributor Agreement](https://cla-assistant.io/Cheshire-Labs/cheshire-drivers);
the CLA bot will prompt you on your first pull request. Under it you assign
copyright in your contribution to Cheshire Labs.

## Development

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Cheshire-Labs/cheshire-drivers.git
cd cheshire-drivers
python -m venv .venv
.venv/Scripts/activate      # Windows
# source .venv/bin/activate # Linux/macOS
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest
```

Use a new virtual environment. The install replaces any upstream PyLabRobot with
our fork, as the README explains.

The test workflow in `.github/workflows/` runs the suite on every pull request
here. A maintainer runs it again in the working repository when porting your
change.

## Coding standards

- [PEP 8](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/), with type hints on every function.
- Clear names over short ones. Small, single-purpose functions.
- Match the style of the code around you.
- Comments explain why, not what. Most code needs none.
- ASCII only in code, tests and strings. Windows consoles crash on the rest.

## Conduct

Be respectful. Disagree about the work, not the person. Maintainers may close
anything that turns abusive.

## Licence

cheshire-drivers is source-available under SSPL-1.0 from 1.0.0 onward; earlier
releases were AGPL-3.0. NOTICE names the copyright holder. Contributions are
accepted under the contributor agreement above.
